          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p>Hi, my name is Trevor Smith. and in this video, I'm gonna be talking about compilers and interpreters. In order to understand compilers and interpreters, I'm first gonna take you through what high level languages and what low level languages are. So a high level language is a language that we can read and we can understand, examples of high level language could be Python, Java, C sharp, you know, the kind of programs which we use on a regular basis.</p>
<p>We can actually type them without too much thought. and as long as we understand the functions and the, the code itself, we have no real issues with it and other people should be able to read it as well, even if they don't have a high understanding of the language. Now, examples of low level language falls into two areas.</p>
<p>One particular areas called assembly language and this is an example of assembly language. As you can see if you had some instruction or some idea what was going on in there, you probably could read it but for the average user, they're just not gonna, they're gonna struggle with this particular language. So they do have their purposes and understanding them is more difficult than say a high level language.</p>
<p>And then there is machine code. Machine code is almost impossible for us to read. Yes, we can see ones and zeros, we can read those machine code is written for machines. The very machine that is actually written for is the processor. As you can see, this is an image of a processor. A processor can understand ones and zeros they understand binary. And that's what they're built for they're built to work with binary. So that is pretty much what high level and low level languages are. Now, when we work with a compiler or an interpreter, what we are doing is we are converting high level language to low level language. Okay, so what is a compiler?</p>
<p>So it translates the entire program from source, which is high level language into machine code. So it takes your entire code and packages it into a file or a multitude of files, which it then can run directly. It does produce an executable file, this is often an exe file and that's the one that you tend to run, but there are some advantages with compiling.</p>
<p>One of them is that the code is fast to produce. Source code remains hidden, so cannot be modified by the customer because it's compiled, it's almost impossible to modify the actual file itself you would have to go back to source, back to the high level language that you were working with. Once it's compiled, it doesn't need a translator it solely it solely works through your operating system, but there are some disadvantages that come with compilers. Compilers use a lot of computer resource, it has to be loaded into the computer's memory at the same time as the source code, so you can imagine you've got your compiler and you've got your source code.</p>
<p>They've got to be both in your memory at the same time. You do need a lot of memory in your computer to compile a program. If you don't, you might not have sufficient memory and this could cause you problems, especially for large projects. It's also difficult to pinpoint errors at the source without accessing the original program before being compiled.</p>
<p>So if you've got a problem with your exe file, you'll need to go back to the pre-compiled file, the high level language, in order to work out, what's gone wrong. So what's an interpreter? Well, interpreters translate each instruction, which is taken in turn and translated into machine code, the instruction is then executed before the next instruction is translated.</p>
<p>Well, there are some advantages with the interpreter. Error messages are output as soon as an error is encountered so it's easy to beat debug. If it's reading line by line and executing each line individually, when it encounters a problem, it'll notify you which line is causing the problem, it's useful for prototypes as the program will run even when part of it has errors, it will operate and function independent to any problems that may be within the rest of the program. There are some disadvantages to using an interpreter, the execution of the program is slow compared to that of a compiled program, so it's reading line by line and passing that information through an interpreter.</p>
<p>Instructions inside a loop have to be translated each time the loop is entered, so if you've got a massive loop, those instructions are gonna continue to loop as well as the loop itself, this is quite inefficient. Now what I'm going to do is I'm going to take you through a few examples so that you understand what's going on.</p>
<p>So I'm gonna show you some examples in Python, and I'm gonna show you some examples in C sharp.</p>

