        <section id="cross-platform-issues"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Cross-platform issues</h2>
              <p>Most software nowadays is designed to work across different computing platforms such as smartphones, servers, tablets, desktop, and laptop computers using the operating systems Mac OS and Microsoft Windows.  Some software requires separate builds for each platform but most will output a file that will work on both platforms.</p>
<p>Developing code that needs to be compatible on different platforms could have cross-platform issue such as:</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          UI / UX considerations
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>User interface (UI) and User experience (UX) are big considerations if the program needs to work across different platforms so there is a consistency with the design and user interaction.  This would include the program resizing correctly with correct placement of items, and the inputs and interactions such as touchscreen controls and or physical buttons which could be used on the different devices.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s3_p1_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Asset availability
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>If there are shared assets that sit outside of the program such as typefaces, then this would be considered.  For example, there are fonts that are available on one operating system but not another.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s3_p1_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Storage
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Storage is a consideration if the program needs to run across different devices as smaller devices, such as a smartphone has less storage. So if the program is large,  this could cause issues and slow it down.</p>
<p>Also, if the program is using cloud-based storage and there is no internet, then issues could occur with data storage.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s3_p1_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Security and compliance
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>If a program is not kept up to date with the operating system that it is on, this could create software vulnerabilities and potential security issues.  This is due to the operating system having regular security updates.</p>
<p>Compliance is another consideration if the program is to be used throughout the world, as different regions have different laws and regulations for data handling.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s3_p1_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Interpreted code
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Interpreted code can essentially be run on any operating system or CPU, providing that there is an interpreter for that system. This means that the programs themselves can be much more 'portable'.  However, a program may take longer to run (than a compiled program), as the code is translated 'line by line' as it is run.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s3_p1_slide5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Compile code
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Compilers must convert source code to a binary, which runs on the specific operating system that they are being compiled for. Compiled programs are therefore not cross-platform, meaning that they cannot be run directly from the source code on incompatible operating systems/CPUs. However, it is possible to generate binaries from the source code which run on different systems using the appropriate compiler.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s3_p1_slide6.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
